1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,320
 So let's talk about other features of Amazon Bedrock.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:03,840
 And of course, if they start appearing

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:06,800
 in the exam a lot more, I will do deep dives on them.

4
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:08,880
 But we have Amazon Bedrock Studio.

5
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:11,760
 This is a UI that you give to your team,

6
00:00:11,760 --> 00:00:15,560
 and that allows your team to use Amazon Bedrock more easily,

7
00:00:15,560 --> 00:00:18,480
 and also to create applications faster.

8
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:21,320
 There's Watermark Detection, which is a feature

9
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:24,640
 of Amazon Bedrock to send an image to it.

10
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:29,640
 And the feature will tell you if this image was generated

11
00:00:29,640 --> 00:00:32,040
 with Amazon Titan or not.

12
00:00:32,040 --> 00:00:34,200
 So now for pricing on Amazon Bedrock,

13
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,280
 so you have the on-demand mode.

14
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:37,920
 And this is where you pay as you go.

15
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,440
 There's no commitment.

16
00:00:39,440 --> 00:00:42,200
 And you're going to get charge for text models

17
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,440
 based on every input and output token processed

18
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:49,520
 for embedding's model, again, for every input token processed.

19
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:51,120
 And for image models, you're going

20
00:00:51,120 --> 00:00:53,600
 to be charged for every image generated.

21
00:00:53,600 --> 00:00:56,120
 This works with the base models only

22
00:00:56,120 --> 00:00:59,440
 that are provided as part of Amazon Bedrock.

23
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,440
 If you want to have some cost savings,

24
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:02,800
 you can use the batch mode.

25
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,840
 So in the batch mode, you can make multiple predictions

26
00:01:04,840 --> 00:01:08,440
 at a time, and the output is going to be a single file

27
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:10,080
 in Amazon S3.

28
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:13,040
 And by using batch mode, you're going to get responses

29
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:15,000
 a bit later than in real time.

30
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,080
 But at least you're going to get discounts of up to 50%.

31
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:22,520
 For provision throughput, this is when you want to purchase

32
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:23,680
 model units for a certain time,

33
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:25,840
 for example, for one month or six month,

34
00:01:25,840 --> 00:01:28,080
 and you're going to get a guaranteed throughput.

35
00:01:28,080 --> 00:01:29,600
 That means that you're going to get

36
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:32,800
 a maximum number of input and output tokens processed

37
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:34,960
 per minute as a guaranteed.

38
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:38,520
 And the idea is that you're going to maintain capacity

39
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:40,280
 and performance, which is very important,

40
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:43,800
 but it does not necessarily provide you with cost savings.

41
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:47,000
 So provision throughput works with base models,

42
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:50,520
 but is necessary if you have a fine tune model

43
00:01:50,520 --> 00:01:53,040
 or custom models or imported models.

44
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:55,360
 In this case, you cannot use on demand.

45
00:01:55,360 --> 00:01:58,240
 You have to use provision throughput.

46
00:01:58,240 --> 00:02:00,520
 So you need to also understand the pricing

47
00:02:00,520 --> 00:02:02,400
 behind improving a model.

48
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:03,600
 So if you use prompt engineering,

49
00:02:03,600 --> 00:02:05,400
 this is when you have techniques

50
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:07,120
 and we'll see them in the next section,

51
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:09,960
 or techniques to improve the prompt in the output of a model,

52
00:02:09,960 --> 00:02:12,840
 well, this requires no further model training.

53
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:15,080
 So there is no additional computation or fine tuning.

54
00:02:15,080 --> 00:02:17,080
 So this is very, very cheap to do.

55
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,160
 If you use rag retrieval augmented generation,

56
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:22,040
 it uses an external knowledge base,

57
00:02:22,040 --> 00:02:24,680
 and because the financial model does not everything.

58
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:25,600
 So it's less complex.

59
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,120
 There is no financial model change.

60
00:02:27,120 --> 00:02:30,160
 You don't need to retrain your model or define tuning,

61
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,280
 but there is a cost, of course,

62
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,280
 because now you need to have a vector database

63
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:35,640
 and you have a system that allows you to access

64
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:37,280
 that vector database.

65
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:40,240
 Then we have instruction-based fine tuning.

66
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:42,920
 So this is when the financial model is fine tune

67
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,480
 with specific instructions,

68
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:45,880
 and that requires additional computation,

69
00:02:45,880 --> 00:02:47,680
 but this is really done to steer

70
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:49,800
 how the model is going to answer

71
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:50,920
 a few important questions,

72
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:52,920
 to set the tone maybe for the model.

73
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:55,800
 And finally, the main adaptation fine tuning

74
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:57,000
 is very expensive,

75
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,320
 because now you're going to adapt the model,

76
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:01,640
 train on the domain specific data sets,

77
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,000
 and then includes creating a lot of data,

78
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,120
 and then retraining them all with all the data,

79
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:09,280
 remember it's unlabeled, whereas instruction-based

80
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:12,120
 was labeled, and this requires intensive computation,

81
00:03:12,120 --> 00:03:15,240
 and so therefore, this intuitively will cost more

82
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,160
 than instruction-based fine tuning.

83
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:20,120
 So how can you do cost savings on Amazon bedrock?

84
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:21,920
 Well, if you use the on-demand pricing model,

85
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,520
 it's going to be great for unpredictable workload,

86
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:26,720
 and you have no long-term commitments.

87
00:03:26,720 --> 00:03:28,200
 If using the batch mode,

88
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:30,480
 you get up to 50% discounts,

89
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:32,400
 but of course, you need to wait a little bit

90
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:33,680
 for your results.

91
00:03:33,680 --> 00:03:38,280
 For provision throughputs, usually it's not a cost-saving measure.

92
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:41,120
 The goal of it is to really reserve capacity

93
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,920
 from AWS and their providers,

94
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:46,560
 and so therefore, you should not use this

95
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:48,760
 as a cost savings strategy.

96
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:50,880
 If you modify the temperature, the top K,

97
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:55,880
 or the top P parameter, you modify how the model is working,

98
00:03:55,880 --> 00:03:58,880
 but this has no impact on the pricing,

99
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:01,520
 and if you have the model size in mind,

100
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:04,840
 usually a smaller model is going to be cheaper,

101
00:04:04,840 --> 00:04:07,840
 but again, this varies on who you get the model from.

102
00:04:07,840 --> 00:04:11,920
 So one of the main driver of cost savings in Amazon bedrock

103
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:15,560
 is to modify the number of input and output tokens.

104
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:16,800
 This is the main driver of cost,

105
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:20,720
 so try to get you prompt as efficiently written as possible,

106
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:24,000
 and try to get you output as concise and short as possible,

107
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,960
 as well, if you are worried about cost savings.

108
00:04:26,960 --> 00:04:27,880
 So that's it for this lecture.

109
00:04:27,880 --> 00:04:28,920
 I hope you liked it,

110
00:04:28,920 --> 00:04:30,840
 and I will see you in the next lecture.

